{
  "teamId": 119,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 Los Angeles Dodgers were a juggernaut from the very first week, a team built on historic power and a pitching staff that made opponents look helpless. They scored 886 runs and allowed only 613, a run differential that screamed dominance, and their 279 home runs set a then-MLB record. From Opening Day through the dog days of summer, this was a team that simply would not stop hitting, with contributions coming from unexpected corners — rookies like Will Smith, bench pieces like Matt Beaty, and resurgent veterans like Russell Martin all delivering when it mattered most.\n\nThe early months established a pattern: the offense would carry the team with towering home runs and clutch extra-base hits, while the bullpen — and Kenley Jansen in particular — provided occasional heart-in-mouth moments. Jansen surrendered multiple devastating blasts throughout the season, including a grand slam to Hunter Renfroe in May and Bryce Harper's crushing double in July, yet the Dodgers kept winning because their lineup was simply too deep and too dangerous to be silenced for long. A 19-7 May and an 18-10 June locked up the NL West before the calendar turned to summer.\n\nThe second half belonged to Cody Bellinger's MVP march and the emergence of Will Smith as a legitimate offensive force behind the plate. Max Muncy continued his power surge, Russell Martin delivered a stunning walk-off single in August, and the lineup rolled through opponents at a 106-win pace. By September, going 18-6 in their final full month, the Dodgers looked like World Series favorites, with every piece clicking at the right time.\n\nThen came Washington, and the magic stopped. The Nationals, who had barely survived their own wild card game, ambushed a Dodger rotation that included Clayton Kershaw and Hyun Jin Ryu. Juan Soto homered twice in the series, Pedro Báez surrendered a three-run bomb from Ryan Zimmerman, and in Game 5 Howie Kendrick broke the game open with a grand slam off Joe Kelly, ending Los Angeles's season with a gut-wrenching NLDS loss. A 106-win season and a record-setting offense were left with nothing but the silence of another October disappointment.",
  "chapters": [
